  "authors": "Xiang-Ping Ding, Xiang-Wen Hu, Shi Chen, Lu Guo",
  "date": "2024",
  "documentType": "case-control study",
  "summary": "This retrospective 1:1 matched case-control study examined risk factors for necrotizing enterocolitis (NEC) in small-for-gestational-age (SGA) neonates admitted over 14 years, identifying neonatal anemia and sepsis as independent risk factors and probiotic prophylaxis as protective.",
  "methods": "• Study design: 1:1 matched retrospective case-control study\n• Data sources: neonatal database, Jiangxi Hospital Affiliated to Children’s Hospital of Chongqing Medical University (Jan 2008–Dec 2022)\n• Sample size: 143 SGA neonates with NEC (Bell stage ≥ II) and 143 matched SGA controls without NEC\n• Analytical methods: Kolmogorov–Smirnov normality test; Student’s t or Mann–Whitney U for continuous variables; χ²/Fisher exact for categorical variables; multivariate logistic regression to identify independent risk factors (P < 0.05 significant)",
  "findings": "• Primary outcomes: neonatal anemia (OR > 1, P < 0.05) and sepsis (OR > 1, P < 0.05) independently increased NEC risk; probiotic prophylaxis was associated with lower NEC incidence\n• Statistical significance: both anemia and sepsis remained significant in multivariate model; protective effect of probiotics also significant\n• Conclusion: anemia and sepsis are key modifiable risk factors for NEC in SGA infants, while routine probiotic administration may reduce incidence; these findings support targeted surveillance and preventive strategies in this high-risk population"
